+# Feature Specification: Create a JSON Patch from two documents
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+### User Story 1 - Send only what changed (P1)
+A client calls `createPatch(from, to)` and gets a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that `applyPatch(from, patch)` turns into a value equal to `to`. Neither input is mutated, and the patch shares no structure with them.
+Acceptance:
+1. Equal documents give an empty patch.
+2. Differences inside nested objects/arrays are described at the deepest path that changed (`add`, `remove`, `replace`); unchanged siblings and the rest of the document are never re-sent.
+3. Differing scalars or types replace the value at that path; a differing root uses path `""`.
+
+### User Story 2 - Array edits stay local (P1)
+1. An item gained or lost produces one `add` / `remove`; items around it are not rewritten.
+2. An item that moves produces a `move`, or a `remove` plus an `add`; items in between are not rewritten.
+3. An item changed in place is patched inside the item (or replaced), not by shifting its neighbours.
+
+### Edge cases
+- Pointer tokens escape `~` as `~0` and `/` as `~1`; empty-string and `__proto__` member names are ordinary data.
+- Object member order is irrelevant; array order is significant; JSON equality as in `applyPatch`'s `test`.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-001 `createPatch(from, to)` is exported from `src/index.ts`.
+- FR-002 `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` equals `to` for any JSON values.
+- FR-003 Inputs are not mutated; the patch contains copies of values.
+- FR-004 The patch is minimal in the senses of User Stories 1 and 2.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- Round-trip holds on randomized documents; single small changes yield a one-operation patch.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Inputs are plain parsed JSON. Only `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move` operations are generated. No dependencies.
